On the one hand, Sobolev's Theorem I.1.3 implies that \(W^{3,\,\alpha}(\Omega)\subset W^{2,\,p}(\Omega)\) with \(1/p=1/\alpha-1/2>0\). Therefore the last term in (3.16) with this \(p\) and \(k=1\) is bounded by:

\[h^{1/2-1/p}\|\,\psi\,\|_{2,\,p,\,\Omega}\leqslant C_{5}h^{1/\beta}\|\,\psi\,\|_{ 3,\,\alpha,\,\Omega}.\]

On the other hand, another application of Theorem I.1.3 yields \(W^{3,\,\alpha}(\Omega)\subset W^{1+2/\beta,\,p}(\Omega)\) for all \(p>0\). Hence (A.26) gives in particular:

\[|\psi\,-\,\hat{P}_{h}\psi|_{1,\,s,\,\Omega}\leqslant C_{6}h^{2/\beta}\,\|\,\psi \,\|_{3,\,\alpha,\,\Omega}.\]

Finally, we infer from Lemma 3.4 that:

\[\|\,\omega-P_{h}\omega\|_{0,\,\Omega}\leqslant C_{7}h^{2/\beta}|\mathrm{ln}(h )|^{1-2/\beta}|\omega|_{1,\,\alpha,\,\Omega}.\]

Thus the dominating power of \(h\) in (3.16) is \(h^{1/\beta}\). This establishes (3.13). Then (3.14) follows again from (2.38). 

_Remark 3.3_.: When \(\alpha=2\), the statement of Theorem 3.2 is still valid for \(l=2\) but not for \(l=1\) because of the last term in (3.16). Instead, like in Remark 3.2 we obtain

\[\|\,u-u_{h}\|_{\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}}\] \[\|\,p-p_{h}\|_{0,\,\Omega}\leqslant C(\varepsilon)h^{1/2- \varepsilon}\begin{cases}\|\,\psi\,\|_{3,\,\Omega}&\quad\forall\varepsilon>0. \\ |p|_{1,\,\Omega}+\|\,\psi\,\|_{3,\,\Omega}&\end{cases}\]

Finally, by using a familiar duality argument, the following theorem completes the statement of Theorem 2.10 and establishes a nearly optimal upper bound for \(|\psi-\psi_{h}|_{1,\,\Omega}\) when \(l=1\).

**Theorem 3.3**.: _Let \(\Omega\) and \(\mathcal{T}_{h}\) be like in Theorem 3.1. Suppose the solution \(\mathbf{u}=\mathbf{curl}\,\psi\) of the Stokes problem (2.1) satisfies:_

\[\psi\,\in H^{k+1}(\Omega)\quad\text{for }2\leqslant k\leqslant l\quad \text{or}\quad\psi\,\in H^{3}(\Omega)\quad\text{if }\,l=1.\]

_Then the solution \(u_{h}=(\mathbf{curl}\,\psi_{h},\omega_{h})\) of Problem (2.29) satisfies the error estimate_:

(3.17) \[|\,\psi\,-\,\psi_{h}|_{1,\,\Omega}\leqslant\begin{cases}Ch^{k}|\psi|_{k+1,\, \Omega}\quad\text{if }2\leqslant k\leqslant l,\\ C(\varepsilon)h^{1-\varepsilon}\|\,\psi\,\|_{3,\,\Omega}\quad\text{if }l=1, \end{cases}\]

_where \(\varepsilon>0\) is arbitrary._

Proof.: When \(l\geqslant 2\), the bound (3.17) is established by Theorem 2.10. When \(l=1\), we use a very similar duality argument. Thus, for each \(\mathbf{g}\,\in\,\mathbf{L}^{2}(\Omega)^{2}\), the auxiliary Stokes problem:

\[(\mathbf{curl}\,\dot{\lambda}_{g},\mathbf{curl}\,\chi)=(\mathbf{g}, \mathbf{curl}\,\chi)\qquad\forall\chi\,\in H^{1}_{0}(\Omega),\] \[(\mathbf{curl}\,\phi_{g},\mathbf{curl}\,\mu)=(\dot{\lambda}_{g}, \mu)\qquad\forall\mu\,\in\,H^{1}(\Omega),\]

has a unique solution \(\dot{\lambda}_{g}\,\in\,H^{1}(\Omega)\), \(\phi_{g}\,\in\,H^{3}(\Omega)\cap H^{1}_{0}(\Omega)\) and

(3.18) \[\|\,\phi_{g}\,\|_{3,\,\Omega}+\,\|\,\dot{\lambda}_{g}\,\|_{1,\,\Omega} \leqslant C_{1}\,\|\,\mathbf{g}\,\|_{0,\,\Omega}.\]
